@page "/projects/vam"

<ProjectPage>

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        Väm is short for <em lang="sv">Var är maten</em>, "where's the food". Gothenburg's
        food trucks post where they'll be on Instagram and Facebook, one post or Story at a
        time, and there has been no single map of them since Streetkäk, the last one, closed in
        2017. Väm is that map. It needs no account and doesn't track anyone. The site is in
        Swedish.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>It's running as a soft launch.</strong> For now, visitors see a page about the
        project, and only I can see the map while I enter and test locations. It opens to
        everyone when the trucks are back for the season, because a map with two trucks on it
        helps nobody. Ten of the eleven planned stages are done.
    </p>

    <h2>The version I threw away</h2>

    <p>
        My first plan was automatic: read the trucks' posts and work out each location from
        the text, with a score for how sure it was. I built most of it before stopping.
    </p>

    <p>
        A program can't read a location that only appears in a picture, a Story or a
        schedule posted as an image, and it can misread the posts it can read. That code is
        archived, and none of it is left in the app.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            The rule that decided it: <strong>a wrong pin costs more than a missing
            one</strong>. The replacement is deliberately simple: I enter every location
            myself, from my phone.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Making it quick to do by hand</h2>

    <p>
        Doing it by hand only works if it's fast. A location can come from a tap on the map,
        a saved spot, a recently used place or the phone's GPS. The app suggests start and end
        times, and won't save a location without them. Each morning, a checklist suggests
        where each truck has been on that weekday in recent weeks. Nothing appears on the map
        until I confirm it.
    </p>

    <p>
        The map is just as honest about what it knows. Only trucks with an entry for today
        are shown, each says when it was last updated, and where a truck has an Instagram
        account, it links there so you can check for yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>Fast and cheap however many people visit</h2>

    <p>
        Visitors' browsers never talk to the database directly. While the map is on screen,
        it checks for updates once a minute. Instead of asking the database each time, the
        website keeps a copy for up to a minute and hands it to everyone who asks in that
        part of the world. In normal use that means the database is asked for fresh data
        about once a minute for each region the site is served from, however many people
        have the map open.
    </p>

    <p>
        The public can't look up where a truck has been in the past; only today's locations
        are shown. The map data itself, the streets and buildings under the pins, is a file
        the project stores itself rather than one fetched from an outside map service.
    </p>

    <h2>Rules the database won't break</h2>

    <p>
        Some mistakes are stopped by the database itself, not by the app remembering to
        check. It won't let one truck be in two places at the same time, and each kind of data
        has its own rules about who may read or change it.
    </p>

    <p>
        I also check that the tests actually work. More than sixty deliberate breakages each
        switch off or weaken one of those safeguards, and for most of them a test must fail.
        A few check something else instead: that a different safeguard still stops the
        mistake, or that the tests refuse to run at all.
    </p>

    <h2>A locked-down admin</h2>

    <p>
        Only one person can change anything: me. Signing in takes a link sent by email and
        then a code from an authenticator app on my phone. I'm signed out automatically after
        twelve hours. Every admin page and every action that changes data repeats these
        checks itself instead of trusting the step before it, and the admin pages get extra
        protection against harmful code an attacker might try to slip into them.
    </p>

    <h2>How it's built</h2>

    <p>
        The code is split into parts that each have one job, and only one of them talks to
        the database. Every change must pass automatic checks before it's accepted. The tests
        run, some of them against a real database running locally. The whole site is put
        together as a trial run. And scans look for known security problems, and for
        passwords or keys saved into the code by mistake.
    </p>

    <p>
        I build it with Claude Code, an AI coding assistant, using a working method that is
        itself part of the project. Plans record every decision and the reason for it, so
        nothing gets debated twice. A log records each time the method let a mistake through,
        so the method can be improved.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
